'''tympany''' (tim′pa̤-ni), ''n.''; pl. ''tympanies'' (-niz). [Formerly also ''timpany''; < OF. ''tympanie'' = Sp. ''timpano'' = Pg. ''tympano'' = It. ''timpano'', < Gr. τυμπανίας, a kind of dropsy in which the belly is stretched like a drum, < τύμπανον, a drum: see ''tympan'', and cf. ''tympanites''.] '''1.''' A swelling out or inflation; an inflated or puffed-up mass or condition; hence, turgidity; bombast; conceit. [Archaic.]

