'''2.''' Something that has a representative or symbolical significance; an emblem, or an emblematic instance.

{{smaller block|Some of our readers may have seen in India a cloud of crows pecking a sick vulture to death — no bad ''type'' of what happens in that country as often as fortune deserts one who has been great and dreaded.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Warren Hastings.}}}}

'''3.''' Specifically, a prefigurement; a foreshadowing of, or that which foreshows, some reality to come, which is called the ''antitype;'' particularly, in ''theol.'', a person, thing, or event in the Old Testament regarded as foreshowing or betokening a corresponding reality of the new dispensation; a prophetic similitude: as, the paschal lamb is the ''type'' of Christ (who is the ''antitype'').

{{smaller block|The nature of ''types'' is in shadow to describe by dark lines a future substance.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), I. 115.}}}}

{{smaller block|As he sees his Day at a distance through ''Types'' and Shadows, he rejoices in it. ''Addison'', Spectator, No. 369.}}

'''4.''' A characteristic embodiment; a definitive example or standard; an exemplar; a pattern; a model.

{{smaller block|<poem>For loftie ''type'' of honour, through the glaunce
Of envies dart, is downe in dust prostrate.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Virgil’s Gnat, l. 557.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>Tophet thence
And black Gehenna call’d, the ''type'' of hell.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 405.}}}}

